  "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cars rented in a day. Since the average rate is $3$ requests per day, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of rented cars can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(3).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}{e}^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that none of the cars are rented the next day is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & =f(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{0}{e}^{-3}}{0!} \\\\  & ≈0.050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
